---
import Section from './Section.astro';
import { me } from '../lib/site';

// TODO(polish): swap in a real headshot at src/assets/personal/headshot.jpg.
// Until then the panel shows the operating-point card instead of a photo.
const facts = [
  ['Degree', me.degree],
  ['School', `${me.school}, ${me.grad}`],
  ['GPA', me.gpa],
  ['Based', me.location],
  ['Pronouns', me.pronouns],
];
---

<Section id="operating" title="Where I sit on the curve.">
  <div class="about">
    <div class="about__text">
      <p class="reveal lede">
        I design the analog piece — the few hundred microwatts that stand between
        a sensor and everything digital downstream.
      </p>

      <p class="reveal muted">
        My focus is biopotential acquisition: EEG and ECG front-ends built in
        180 nm CMOS, running deep in subthreshold where a transistor's
        transconductance is set by a bias current measured in nanoamperes. It is
        an unforgiving place to work. Gain, noise, offset and power all pull
        against each other, the models are least trustworthy exactly where you
        are operating, and nothing is fixable in software afterwards.
      </p>

      <p class="reveal muted">
        I care about it because of what it enables. Seizure monitoring still
        largely means a hospital visit and a cap of wet electrodes. Getting the
        same signal quality out of something you can wear all day is not a
        software problem — it is a circuit that has to be quiet, small and
        nearly free to run. That constraint is the interesting part.
      </p>

      <p class="reveal muted">
        Right now I am a research assistant in WPI's Integrated Circuits and
        Systems Lab working on wearable and physiological sensing, and a teaching
        assistant for Microelectronics I. I co-founded WPI's chapter of the IEEE
        Solid-State Circuits Society because there was no room on campus where
        people talked about this work, and now there is.
      </p>

      <p class="reveal muted">
        This past summer I was at MIT Lincoln Laboratory, on the RF transmitter
        team at the Lincoln Space Surveillance Complex — building hardware to
        replace obsolete equipment on the Millstone Hill Radar, which has been
        running and evolving since 1957. My favourite part was that the work is
        never finished: every upgrade is the starting point for the next one.
        Schematics, boards, chassis, and a lot of time on a spectrum analyser
        finding out how the thing I designed actually behaves. That gap, between
        the simulation and the bench, is the part of engineering I want more of.
      </p>
    </div>

    <aside class="about__card reveal">
      <p class="stat__k">Operating point</p>
      <dl>
        {facts.map(([k, v]) => (
          <div><dt>{k}</dt><dd>{v}</dd></div>
        ))}
      </dl>
      <a class="btn" href={me.resume} download>Download CV</a>
    </aside>
  </div>
</Section>

<style>
  .about {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 18rem;
    gap: 3rem;
    align-items: start;
  }
  @media (max-width: 880px) { .about { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 2.5rem; } }

  .about__text { display: grid; gap: 1.1rem; max-width: 60ch; }
  .about__text .lede { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; color: var(--fg); }

  .about__card {
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-top: 2px solid var(--accent);
    border-radius: 4px;
    background: var(--bg-2);
    padding: 1.5rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.25rem;
    position: sticky;
    top: 5rem;
  }
  @media (max-width: 880px) { .about__card { position: static; } }

  .about__card dl { display: grid; gap: 0.85rem; margin: 0; }
  .about__card dl > div { display: grid; gap: 0.15rem; }
  .about__card dt {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.62rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--fg-3);
  }
  .about__card dd { margin: 0; font-size: 0.92rem; }
  .about__card .btn { justify-content: center; }
</style>
